{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>The Rock, His work is perfect.</b> The explanation is: because he first said that the occupation with Torah brings rain and dew, therefore he gave notice that if a person occupies himself with Torah yet his livelihood is difficult and there is no bread, or if he sees a person who does not bear the yoke of Torah and despises those who study it, yet they measure out to him with a coarse measure — eating and being satiated and having surplus — contrary to the promises spoken concerning those who study Torah, then because of this he should not rebel and heresy should not be born in him, heaven forbid. Rather, he should justify the judgment upon himself and say, HaShem is true and His judgment is a judgment of truth. And this is what the verse says — <b>הַצּוּר</b> — meaning a term of might and strength, and this will point to the hand of HaShem ruling over man. Nevertheless, you should justify and say <b>תָּמִים פָּעֳלוֹ</b> — this corresponds to what one sees, that His way is to measure goodness with a coarse measure yet they measured for him with a fine one. And He concludes, <b>״כִּי כָל דְּרָכָיו מִשְׁפָּט״</b> — this includes also the case where His way is to measure goodness with a fine measure yet they measured for him with a coarse one, <b>״כִּי כָל דְּרָכָיו מִשְׁפָּט״</b>, for He does not bring suffering upon the righteous until he has incurred liability for it, and He does not bring goodness upon the wicked unless they have earned it according to their deeds." "And He concludes, <b>״אֵל אֱמוּנָה״</b> etc., and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sifrei): ״אֵל אֱמוּנָה״ — to pay the righteous their reward in the World to Come; ״וְאֵין עָוֶל״ — to pay the wicked their reward in this world. And the intention of this statement is according to their words which they said (Shocher Tov on Psalm 4) concerning the verse ״נָתַתָּ שִׂמְחָה בְלִבִּי מֵעֵת דְּגָנָם וְתִירוֹשָׁם רָבּוּ״ (Psalms 4:8) — that David said: from the time he saw the tranquility of the wicked, he drew an a fortiori inference about himself: if for these, who transgress His will, it is thus, etc. And this is what the verse says — ״אֵל אֱמוּנָה״ — meaning, the righteous will justify the faith of his reward in HaShem when he sees the measure of ״וְאֵין עָוֶל,״ which is the reward of the wicked in this world for the slight merit they possess." "<b>״צַדִּיק וְיָשָׁר הוּא.״</b> The explanation is: for the Master, HaShem of Hosts, created the world with judgment, as it is written (Proverbs 29:4) ״בְּמִשְׁפָּט יַעֲמִיד אָרֶץ,״ and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tanhuma, Shofetim) that HaShem swore that the world would stand on judgment. But we find thirteen attributes, all of them mercy — and if He swore upon judgment, what place is there for mercy?" "Rather, the order is on this path: for at first, when He sits upon judgment, He establishes the judgment upon its pillar; but when He comes to make payment, He is appeased with mercy and reconciled with supplications. And it is in this vein that the verse says (Psalms 145:17) ״צַדִּיק ה׳ בְּכָל דְּרָכָיו וְחָסִיד בְּכָל מַעֲשָׂיו״ — meaning, when He comes to guide a matter, He guides it along the line of judgment; but בְּכָל מַעֲשָׂיו — meaning, at the time of action, when a person comes and pleads — He is an El compassionate and gracious and has mercy upon him. And this is what the verse says — <b>״צַדִּיק וְיָשָׁר הוּא״</b> — meaning, righteous along the line of judgment, as Rashi explains regarding what they said in the Gemara (Avodah Zarah 6a): ״ודילמא נח טרפה הוה״, and he answers ״תָּמִים כְתִיב בֵּיהּ,״ and they press ״ודלמא צַדִּיק בִּדְרָכָיו תָּמִים בְּמַעֲשָׂיו״, and Rashi explained ״צַדִּיק בִּדְרָכָיו עַל פִּי הַדִּין״ etc. — and this is at first; but afterward ״וְיָשָׁר״ — meaning, upright beyond the line of the law, as they of blessed memory said (Bava Metzi'a 108b) in explanation of the verse ״וְעָשִׂיתָ הַיָּשָׁר וְהַטּוֹב״ (Deuteronomy 6:18, and Rashi ad loc.)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {1 "Resolved מֵעֵת as 'from the time'; the underlying midrashic argument is that David infers from the wicked's prosperity how much more so the righteous will be rewarded."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:21:21.024883873Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}